<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP Dependency-Check suppression file.
  https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/general/suppression.html

  This file exists to unblock CONFIRMED FALSE POSITIVES only — a real CVE in a
  dependency we can't upgrade yet belongs in a tracked issue with a version
  bump plan, not here. Every <suppress> entry below must have:
    1. A comment linking to the issue/PR that reviewed it and explains why
       it's a false positive (wrong CPE match, vendored/shaded code that
       doesn't ship the vulnerable class, etc.) or why the risk is accepted
       (with an expiry date via `until`).
    2. As narrow a <packageUrl>/<cve> match as possible — never suppress an
       entire dependency's CVEs with a blanket regex unless the finding
       explains why that's necessary.

-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">
  <!--
    False positive, wrong CPE match (reviewed in the 2.21.0 release PR, #259). Spring AI's jars
    are version 2.0.1, and the analyzer matched that version against VMware's discontinued
    "VMware Server 2.0.1" hypervisor (cpe:2.3:a:vmware:server), attaching its 2009–2010 CVEs
    (CVE-2009-3732, CVE-2010-1141, …). Spring AI is not VMware Server and contains none of that
    code. Only that CPE is suppressed: cpe:2.3:a:vmware:spring_ai still reports any real
    Spring AI CVE.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.ai/.*$</packageUrl>
    <cpe>cpe:/a:vmware:server</cpe>
  </suppress>
</suppressions>
